WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.111 --> 00:00:05.313
<v Daniel>So for the last three years, pretty much every career guru has told you the exact same thing.</v>

2
00:00:05.613 --> 00:00:07.373
<v Maya>Yeah, that you need to learn to be nice.</v>

3
00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:16.696
<v Daniel>Right, like focus on your empathy, you know, double down on your people skills. Because the robots are coming for the spreadsheets and the coding, but they can't take our humanity.</v>

4
00:00:17.077 --> 00:00:20.218
<v Maya>Right, it's the ultimate comforting narrative for the modern workforce.</v>

5
00:00:20.658 --> 00:00:27.520
<v Daniel>But today we're bringing you the receipts from, well, over 11 million job postings. And it turns out...</v>

6
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:32.914
<v Maya>It turns out that betting blindly on being nice might actually get you automated out of a job.</v>

7
00:00:33.035 --> 00:00:33.275
Yeah.

8
00:00:34.176 --> 00:00:43.284
<v Daniel>Uncomfortable, right? But our mission today is to kind of cut through all that LinkedIn thought leadership noise and extract the real insights from a stack of recent economic research.</v>

9
00:00:43.664 --> 00:00:53.053
<v Maya>Exactly. Because we need to uncover exactly which human skills are being actively de-prioritized in the Gen AI era and, you know, which ones are genuinely surviving.</v>

10
00:00:53.445 --> 00:01:03.887
<v Daniel>And to do that, we are anchoring this deep dive on a massive, really revealing paper. It was published online in the journal Management Science on July 24, 2026.</v>

11
00:01:04.287 --> 00:01:16.970
<v Maya>Yeah, it's by Devajili, Yilmaz, Gaba, and Grieve from INSEAD. And the title really just says it all. It's called Skill Deprioritization, Reorganizing in the Age of Generative Artificial Intelligence.</v>

12
00:01:17.432 --> 00:01:25.665
<v Daniel>So I have to ask you straight up because everyone tells you to lean into your social skills to survive this wave. Are you telling me that's actually bad advice?</v>

13
00:01:26.246 --> 00:01:30.173
<v Maya>I mean, based on the actual hiring behavior of firms, yes, it is.</v>

14
00:01:30.572 --> 00:01:31.612
<v Daniel>Wow. Yeah.</v>

15
00:01:32.232 --> 00:01:47.096
<v Maya>Yeah. Some of the deeply human skills of organizing and coordinating people are just being rapidly deprioritized. Meanwhile, a very sharp, almost counterintuitive set of traits is taking their place. But, you know, to understand why the conventional wisdom</v>

16
00:01:47.176 --> 00:01:52.917
<v Maya>is missing the mark, we really need to look at how these INSEAD researchers actually tracked this shift.</v>

17
00:01:53.244 --> 00:02:00.689
<v Daniel>Because they didn't just send out a survey asking CEOs how they felt about AI, right? They went looking for the actual DNA of the modern job.</v>

18
00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:15.719
<v Maya>Right. They looked at massive empirical behavioral data. I'm talking 1,820 publicly listed U.S. firms and covering 40,465 firm months, which in real terms translates to</v>

19
00:02:15.779 --> 00:02:19.361
<v Maya>about 11.15 million light cast job postings.</v>

20
00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:26.630
<v Daniel>That is a staggering amount of text to analyze. And the timeline they chose is what makes this a true natural experiment, right?</v>

21
00:02:26.971 --> 00:02:36.583
<v Maya>It's the perfect window. Yeah. They looked at the 12 months leading up to and then the 12 months immediately following the release of ChatGPT on November 30th, 2022.</v>

22
00:02:36.783 --> 00:02:38.786
<v Daniel>So exactly before and after the shock.</v>

23
00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:53.700
<v Maya>Exactly. And they used a classic difference in differences design. So they compared a treatment group of 966 firms and industries that had higher Gen AI exposure against a control group of 854 lower exposure firms.</v>

24
00:02:54.181 --> 00:02:57.203
<v Daniel>So how did they decide, like, who was actually exposed and who wasn't?</v>

25
00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:07.328
<v Maya>Well, they used the established Felton et al. language modeling measure, which basically just calculates how much of an industry's typical work can be done by a large language model.</v>

26
00:03:07.508 --> 00:03:08.029
<v Daniel>Right, okay.</v>

27
00:03:08.409 --> 00:03:18.418
<v Maya>And then they took 33,832 individual skills from those millions of job ads, and they sorted them into a whole organizing framework.</v>

28
00:03:19.038 --> 00:03:20.740
<v Daniel>So they bucketed them into categories.</v>

29
00:03:20.994 --> 00:03:32.220
<v Maya>Yes. Based on fundamental organizational problems, they grouped them into task division, task allocation, information provision, reward distribution, and exception management.</v>

30
00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:44.846
<v Daniel>It really is like sequencing the corporate genome. Instead of just looking at a job title, they ripped open the descriptions to see exactly which traits employers were suddenly trying to edit out after JetGPT hit the market.</v>

31
00:03:45.326 --> 00:03:46.247
<v Maya>That's a great way to put it.</v>

32
00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:51.611
<v Daniel>So let's get to the hard numbers. What exactly was being deleted in those highly exposed firms?</v>

33
00:03:52.311 --> 00:04:02.939
<v Maya>The drops are honestly dramatic. Let me give you the most quotable findings right from the paper. In exposed firms, job postings asking for monitoring skills fell by 23.2%. Wow. Yeah.</v>

34
00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.020
<v Daniel>Over a fifth.</v>

35
00:04:05.581 --> 00:04:11.805
<v Maya>Yeah. Task division fell by 23%. Asynchronous communication dropped 21.4%. Coordination fell by 21.3%.</v>

36
00:04:16.284 --> 00:04:19.036
<v Daniel>This is wild. It's a bloodbath for middle management.</v>

37
00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:26.995
<v Maya>It really is. And operational exception handling went down by 19% and synchronous communication fell by 18.5%. I</v>

38
00:04:27.155 --> 00:04:41.581
<v Daniel>mean, 20% drops in coordination and monitoring in a single year. That's a direct hit to the traditional management toolkit. But I want to pause and talk about the how here. Like it's easy to say task division dropped 23%.</v>

39
00:04:42.121 --> 00:04:48.444
<v Daniel>But what does that actually look like in an office? How does an AI replace a manager dividing tasks?</v>

40
00:04:48.937 --> 00:05:03.245
<v Maya>Well, think about a software development team, right? Historically, a project manager would read through the overnight updates, assess the backlog, look at everyone's schedule, and spend like two hours deciding who gets which ticket. That is task division and asynchronous communication.</v>

41
00:05:04.065 --> 00:05:05.046
<v Daniel>Okay, and now?</v>

42
00:05:06.026 --> 00:05:17.192
<v Maya>Now, an AI agent digests the Slack threads, analyzes the code commits, and just auto-assigns the JIRA tickets based on developer bandwidth. The manager doesn't need to coordinate that anymore. They just hit approve.</v>

43
00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:23.743
<v Daniel>So the routine work of simply passing information from person A to person B is just evaporating.</v>

44
00:05:23.983 --> 00:05:33.709
<v Maya>Exactly. And that leads to a major structural shift. The authors noted that exposed firms started posting fewer managerial openings relative to non-managerial ones.</v>

45
00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:35.841
<v Daniel>Ah, so the ratios are changing.</v>

46
00:05:36.062 --> 00:05:48.852
<v Maya>Right. It points to wider spans of control. A manager who used to handle, say, five direct reports because they spent all day monitoring them, well, now they can handle 15 because the AI is doing all the routine monitoring and status updating.</v>

47
00:05:49.313 --> 00:06:04.133
<v Daniel>Hold on, though. I got to play devil's advocate here. We're talking about millions of job ads, sure, but these are just wish lists that HR puts on a website. I mean, nobody's actually getting fired in this specific data set, right? Isn't it a bit early to declare mental management</v>

48
00:06:04.213 --> 00:06:06.973
<v Daniel>dead just because some keywords dropped off a job portal?</v>

49
00:06:08.074 --> 00:06:15.936
<v Maya>That is a completely fair challenge. And we have to state the limits of this study out loud because the authors are actually incredibly transparent about this.</v>

50
00:06:15.956 --> 00:06:17.256
<v Daniel>Okay. Lay them on me.</v>

51
00:06:17.756 --> 00:06:25.578
<v Maya>First, these are early signals of what firms expect to need rather than absolute proof of a finalized, realized reorganization.</v>

52
00:06:26.036 --> 00:06:30.819
<v Daniel>Right. So it's text on a screen, not a payroll ledger or actual layoffs or wages.</v>

53
00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:44.667
<v Maya>Exactly. Furthermore, the treatment in this study is measured at the industry level by exposure. It's not tracking a specific firm's actual integrated adoption of AI. It captures the shift in hiring priorities broadly.</v>

54
00:06:44.687 --> 00:06:45.848
<v Daniel>Okay. That makes sense.</v>

55
00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:57.126
<v Maya>We also have to remember, we are only looking at large public U.S. firms here. And the data window ends in November 2023. So we are only capturing that very first year of the shock.</v>

56
00:06:57.686 --> 00:07:09.495
<v Daniel>Okay. So if coordinating, monitoring, and passing information are clearly on the chopping block in these ads, what skills showed no significant change? Like, what survived the edit?</v>

57
00:07:09.755 --> 00:07:23.135
<v Maya>So the skills that showed no significant change in demand were staffing... mapping, designing incentives, and conflict resolution. And we actually saw the effects in several other categories grow even stronger after the release of GPT-4.</v>

58
00:07:23.495 --> 00:07:38.399
<v Daniel>So here is where it gets really interesting. Firms are perfectly happy to hand over the routine tracking of people to AI, but they won't hand over dealing with people in conflict or deciding what rewards are fair. It's like AI is the new hall monitor,</v>

59
00:07:38.439 --> 00:07:41.700
<v Daniel>but you still need a human principle for the heavy disciplinary stuff.</v>

60
00:07:42.207 --> 00:07:53.792
<v Maya>The authors do lean toward that interpretation, yeah. The idea that human judgment remains highly valued is a very tempting contrast. But, and this is important, I have to issue a strong reality check here.</v>

61
00:07:54.272 --> 00:07:54.993
<v Daniel>Okay, go for it.</v>

62
00:07:55.513 --> 00:08:05.597
<v Maya>In a statistical model, no significant change is absolutely not proof of safety. The authors treat it as a hypothesis that the data only weakly supports.</v>

63
00:08:06.037 --> 00:08:11.740
<v Daniel>Wait, break that down for me. If the data says the demand for conflict resolution didn't drop, why isn't it safe?</v>

64
00:08:12.146 --> 00:08:26.970
<v Maya>Because you have to look at the math beneath the headline. The point estimate in the main table for conflict resolution is actually negative 0.224. Oh, wow. Yeah, that coefficient is about as large as the ones that are statistically significant, but it</v>

65
00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.611
<v Maya>comes with a very wide standard error.</v>

66
00:08:28.751 --> 00:08:30.271
<v Daniel>Meaning the data is messy.</v>

67
00:08:30.471 --> 00:08:45.056
<v Maya>Right. It's imprecise. The honest reading is that it is imprecise, not zero. You can't just assume it's zero. In fact, under one alternative definition in the paper where they focus specifically on non-routine cognitive industries, conflict resolution actually</v>

68
00:08:45.216 --> 00:08:46.596
<v Maya>does show a negative effect.</v>

69
00:08:46.896 --> 00:08:54.919
<v Daniel>Wow. So even the human principle might be on shaky ground. So we definitely cannot tell people that their conflict skills are proven bulletproof.</v>

70
00:08:55.160 --> 00:09:06.957
<v Maya>We absolutely cannot. And there are other caveats, too, like that drop in synchronous communication we mentioned. That isn't robust in every single model specification, and it actually loses significance with continuous exposure.</v>

71
00:09:07.478 --> 00:09:11.243
<v Daniel>It sounds like firms might just be grabbing the low-hanging fruit first, right?</v>

72
00:09:11.529 --> 00:09:26.256
<v Maya>The authors predict exactly that. They suggest that task allocation, incentives, and conflict resolution might just be second-order reorganizations. So firms cut the easy coordination tasks first. Later, once they figure out how to really</v>

73
00:09:26.476 --> 00:09:30.678
<v Maya>integrate AI deeper, those heavier judgment tasks might face the cuts too.</v>

74
00:09:31.164 --> 00:09:44.436
<v Daniel>Okay, so the NCAID paper shows companies actively cutting coordination and communication skills from their job ads. But if we take that as gospel, we kind of crash headfirst into a massive contradiction found in our supporting sources.</v>

75
00:09:44.936 --> 00:09:52.984
<v Maya>We do, and it is a defining disagreement in the current research landscape. Let's look at a study by Fleck and colleagues from August 2026. Okay,</v>

76
00:09:53.885 --> 00:09:54.345
<v Daniel>what did they do?</v>

77
00:09:54.651 --> 00:10:07.588
<v Maya>They ran a discrete choice experiment with 992 German firms. And in that setup, employers explicitly stated they would pay up to 15% above the average salary for a candidate with high social skills.</v>

78
00:10:08.089 --> 00:10:08.710
<v Daniel>A 15% premium?</v>

79
00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:24.614
<v Maya>Yes. They also stated they'd pay about 2% above average for high prompting skills, which jumps to a 9% hiring boost in large or AI-adopting firms. Though they noted prompting skills do not make up for occupation-specific gaps. You still have to know how to do the</v>

80
00:10:24.694 --> 00:10:25.295
<v Maya>actual job.</v>

81
00:10:25.775 --> 00:10:40.459
<v Daniel>Obviously. But so what does this all mean? I mean, on one hand, we have 11 million job ads proving firms are actively stripping coordination roles out of their hiring profiles. On the other hand, when you ask employers directly, they claim they will pay a 15%</v>

82
00:10:40.499 --> 00:10:45.481
<v Daniel>premium for social skills. Are they lying or do they just not know what they want?</v>

83
00:10:45.793 --> 00:10:52.976
<v Maya>Well, I wouldn't call it lying. It's a massive definitional problem. The phrase social skills is simply too big and too</v>

84
00:10:52.996 --> 00:10:53.936
<v Daniel>vague.</v>

85
00:10:54.336 --> 00:11:09.181
<v Maya>What the INSEAD job ad data shows falling is routine information passing, monitoring, and basic coordination. AI can do those perfectly well. What the employers in the German study are likely valuing is complex judgment among people. And remember, that</v>

86
00:11:09.241 --> 00:11:13.323
<v Maya>German study is stated preference, not real hiring or realized wages.</v>

87
00:11:13.684 --> 00:11:23.407
<v Daniel>Basically, passing a memo is technically a social skill, but so is negotiating a hostage release. An AI can summarize the memo, but you wouldn't send an AI to negotiate with kidnappers.</v>

88
00:11:23.847 --> 00:11:38.431
<v Maya>That is a very visceral way to put it, but yeah, it's accurate. To truly settle this debate, we can't just compare broad stated preferences from a survey against early job ad text. We would need long term data on actual realized wages and hiring broken down across</v>

89
00:11:38.731 --> 00:11:41.132
<v Maya>very specific subcategories of social skills.</v>

90
00:11:42.009 --> 00:11:50.112
<v Daniel>To figure out which specific type of social skill really matters right now, we can look at how jobs are actually shifting day to day for the people who manage to keep them, right?</v>

91
00:11:50.612 --> 00:12:00.456
<v Maya>Yes. And this is where a paper by Gulati and colleagues provides a huge missing link. This is an August 2026 version of a paper that was first posted on Archive back in March 2025. So</v>

92
00:12:00.636 --> 00:12:02.897
<v Daniel>it's been around for a minute, but it's crucial.</v>

93
00:12:03.217 --> 00:12:11.060
<v Maya>Exactly. They looked at 7 million postings from 595 firms, specifically comparing roles that adopted Gen AI with roles that did not.</v>

94
00:12:11.774 --> 00:12:15.196
<v Daniel>And what did they find about the demand for social skills down in the trenches?</v>

95
00:12:15.997 --> 00:12:22.521
<v Maya>They found that relative demand for social skills actually fell by 3.4% while cognitive skills held steady.</v>

96
00:12:23.241 --> 00:12:27.324
<v Daniel>Wait, wait. Social skills fell relative to cognitive skills?</v>

97
00:12:27.984 --> 00:12:28.144
<v Maya>Yep.</v>

98
00:12:28.645 --> 00:12:33.548
<v Daniel>But that is the exact opposite of what the empathy is everything crowd has been preaching. Why did it fall?</v>

99
00:12:34.417 --> 00:12:49.300
<v Maya>Because of how the boundaries of a job are expanding. The fall in social skills was concentrated heavily among non-managers. What the researchers saw was individual contributors adding finance, HR, and legal skills to their own roles. They are serving themselves.</v>

100
00:12:50.060 --> 00:13:03.423
<v Daniel>Ah, so it's a self-service revolution. Like, if I'm a marketing manager and I need a standard vendor contract drafted, I used to have to email legal, set up a meeting, coordinate with their timeline, and pass documents back and forth.</v>

101
00:13:04.062 --> 00:13:06.323
<v Maya>Right, which took a lot of social coordination.</v>

102
00:13:06.663 --> 00:13:13.105
<v Daniel>Exactly. But now I just have an AI draft the contract, check it against company policy, and I'm done in five minutes.</v>

103
00:13:13.885 --> 00:13:28.729
<v Maya>You've basically become a one-person multi-department. And because you don't need to coordinate with specialists as often, your need for routine social skills goes down, but your technical and administrative reach goes up. The authors of this paper explicitly warn that betting on social</v>

104
00:13:28.769 --> 00:13:32.570
<v Maya>skills as protection against automation really needs to be reconsidered.</v>

105
00:13:33.233 --> 00:13:42.496
<v Daniel>Okay, so if routine coordination is dead and simply being a friendly team player doesn't protect you, what kind of human traits actually pay off in this new environment?</v>

106
00:13:42.956 --> 00:13:52.439
<v Maya>Let's look at a May 2026 meta-analysis from the World Bank by Angel Urdanola and colleagues. Now, to be clear, this paper measures no AI effect directly.</v>

107
00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:53.700
<v Daniel>Right, no AI effect.</v>

108
00:13:54.391 --> 00:14:07.680
<v Maya>Instead, it looks deeply at the wage premium of personality traits. They found that a one standard deviation increase in a big five personality trait goes with 1.4 to 3.1 percent higher wages.</v>

109
00:14:08.741 --> 00:14:11.102
<v Daniel>OK, so which trait pays the highest premium?</v>

110
00:14:11.923 --> 00:14:14.345
<v Maya>The highest return is to lower agreeableness.</v>

111
00:14:15.125 --> 00:14:19.388
<v Daniel>Lower agreeableness? You mean being stubborn and difficult to work with?</v>

112
00:14:19.978 --> 00:14:34.542
<v Maya>Not difficult, but assertive. In a professional context, lower agreeableness translates to assertiveness and the willingness to challenge consensus. And importantly, they note that training can raise these traits by 0.46 standard deviations on average, so</v>

113
00:14:34.882 --> 00:14:35.462
<v Maya>they can be taught.</v>

114
00:14:36.082 --> 00:14:51.078
<v Daniel>So being nice is not the same as being skilled with people. The social skills that survived the AI transition aren't about just getting along and passing memos. They are about having the backbone to challenge the consensus, push back on bad ideas, and</v>

115
00:14:51.138 --> 00:14:55.803
<v Daniel>exercise independent judgment, which, thankfully, is a behavior you can actually learn.</v>

116
00:14:56.744 --> 00:15:05.052
<v Maya>It's a completely different paradigm for professional development. And this brings us to a critical juncture for how we prepare people for this landscape, especially when we look at higher education.</v>

117
00:15:05.837 --> 00:15:18.685
<v Daniel>Yeah, if you're looking at the blueprint for a university, the implications are massive. If routine coordination is being automated away and self-serve administration and assertiveness are king, how do we prepare the next generation of the workforce?</v>

118
00:15:19.406 --> 00:15:34.075
<v Maya>Universities need to fundamentally pivot what they teach. They need to stop teaching students how to write status reports and run basic coordination routines. The curriculum has to shift toward teaching intense conflict resolution, high-stakes negotiation, and the fair ethical judgment of</v>

119
00:15:34.135 --> 00:15:34.435
<v Maya>people.</v>

120
00:15:35.385 --> 00:15:48.583
<v Daniel>But here's the credentialing problem. How do you put willingness to challenge consensus on a college transcript? Right now, an A in a seminar usually means you are highly agreeable, you agreed with a professor, and you followed the syllabus perfectly.</v>

121
00:15:49.063 --> 00:16:03.891
<v Maya>Exactly. It requires an entirely new model of assessment. A transcript just doesn't show assertiveness. Universities are going to face an urgent need to figure out how to credential these hidden, spiky skills. They will likely need to partner directly with employers to</v>

122
00:16:03.931 --> 00:16:10.575
<v Maya>create simulations or live-stakes projects to prove students actually possess the ability to negotiate and judge fairly.</v>

123
00:16:11.278 --> 00:16:16.221
<v Daniel>Because if they don't, we are going to see some very vulnerable populations left entirely behind.</v>

124
00:16:16.461 --> 00:16:29.869
<v Maya>Right. The INSED paper doesn't measure this demographic impact directly, but we can make a very clear inference from the data. The people most exposed to this shift are early career workers, adult learners, and first-generation students.</v>

125
00:16:30.633 --> 00:16:37.518
<v Daniel>Because coordinating, reporting, and routine information passing have historically been the entry-level rungs on the corporate ladder.</v>

126
00:16:37.718 --> 00:16:52.350
<v Maya>Yes. If AI wipes out those coordination roles, how do those specific groups get their foot in the door? I mean, you can't just walk into a Fortune 500 company and start doing high-level conflict resolution on day one. You usually build that capital and trust by being a</v>

127
00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:53.971
<v Maya>reliable coordinator first.</v>

128
00:16:54.572 --> 00:17:08.967
<v Daniel>So to pull all these threads together, the AI wave isn't just coming for the coders and the data analysts. It is actively reshaping the management layer. It is cutting out routine coordination by over 20% in exposed firms.</v>

129
00:17:09.628 --> 00:17:18.257
<v Daniel>It's expanding the span of control. And it's demanding a much sharper, more assertive definition of what we traditionally called people skills.</v>

130
00:17:19.085 --> 00:17:33.755
<v Maya>Which really leaves us with the INSEAD author's own biggest worry, the long run hollowing out of expertise. If AI wipes out the entry level coordination roles and thins out the middle management layer, the promotion ladder loses its middle rungs.</v>

131
00:17:34.395 --> 00:17:42.901
<v Daniel>Yeah. How does the workforce of tomorrow build deep contextual expertise if the training ground of middle management no longer exists?</v>

132
00:17:43.503 --> 00:17:51.991
<v Maya>It's a structural crisis in the making. If the high ground of routine coordination is sinking, we have to figure out how to build better boats for the next generation of workers.</v>

133
00:17:53.052 --> 00:17:58.778
<v Daniel>A huge, provocative thought. Definitely something for you to mull over as you navigate the future of your own work.</v>
